Now that is an interesting comparison. I played chess since I was four 
and was a member of the US Chess Federation for years, playing in large 
tournaments. I would say that chess is much more difficult to master.

The compelling factor is that the body of knowledge surrounding chess is 
vast. People spend a lot of time trying to get down the basics, then 
progressing to endgames (rook and king vs rook king and pawn) to 
complicated endgames (bishop and x number of pawns and king, knight and 
x number of pawns, for instance) to middlegames to the truly complicated 
subject of openings.

Some years ago I owned a book by a British grandmaster on the Najdorf 
variation of the Sicilian Defense (1. e4 c4 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 
Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 - for those who can read algebraic notation in chess).

For those who do not play, this is one single variation of one single 
method of defense to a particular first move from white - moving the 
king's pawn forward by two spaces. The book on this seemingly trivial 
subject in chess was well over 400 pages long, with many diagrams and 
pages filled with notations and top-level games played against strong 
grandmasters through the years. I bought the books hoping to find a 
suitable counter to opponents using this defense against me and also to 
use it against my opponents.

Of course, I also needed to learn countermeasures against an opponent 
playing the Caro-Kann defense, the French defense, the Counter-Center 
gambit, and variations that rise from black playing e5 in response to e4.

Note that this knowledge was only needed in half my games - when I 
played with the white pieces. The knowledge needed to succeed when 
playing with black is similar but requires a different perspective and 
is correspondingly huge.

Thousands of books have been written on chess, and rightly so. Certainly 
while Nethack is a worthy intellectual pursuit, it is not as difficult 
to master as chess.

